There isn't one best way to brew coffee. Each method makes a different cup, and each one asks for a different amount of effort, time and money. The right one depends on what you like to drink and how much you want to fuss with it in the morning.

The basics from [Brewing Basics](/coffee/guides/brewing-basics/) (ratio, grind, water and tasting) apply to all of them. This series is about what makes each method different.

This is the overview. Each method has its own guide with a recipe to start from.

## The methods side by side

<CoffeeBrewMethodChart />

## Immersion vs. pour-through

Most methods fall into one of two groups, and knowing which one you're using explains a lot about the cup.

- **Immersion** methods (French press, cold brew) soak the grounds in water for the whole brew. They're forgiving and make a heavier, fuller cup.
- **Pour-through** methods (pour-over, drip machines) let water flow through a bed of grounds and out the bottom. They're a little more sensitive to grind and pouring, and they make a cleaner, brighter cup, especially with a paper filter.

Then there are the odd ones. The **AeroPress** is a bit of immersion, a bit of pour-over and a bit of pressure. **Espresso** uses a lot of pressure to push water through very finely ground coffee in about half a minute, which is why it's its own thing entirely.

## The filter matters too

Paper filters catch most of the coffee oils and the fine particles, so the cup tastes cleaner and lighter. Metal filters (like a French press screen) let them through, so the cup is heavier, richer and a little cloudy. Neither is better. It's one of the biggest reasons a French press and a pour-over taste so different, even with the same beans.

## If you're not sure where to start

- **You want the best cup for the least money:** [Pour-over](/coffee/guides/pour-over-coffee/). It's inexpensive, and it only takes a little practice to get good.
- **You want simple and quick, and you like a full-bodied cup:** [French press](/coffee/guides/french-press-coffee/).
- **You want simple and good, and you like to tinker:** [AeroPress](/coffee/guides/aeropress-coffee/).
- **You drink iced coffee, or want coffee ready in the fridge:** [Cold brew](/coffee/guides/cold-brew-coffee/).
- **You're making coffee for a crowd:** A good [drip machine](/coffee/guides/drip-coffee-machines/).
- **You want it fast and the same every time:** [Capsule machines](/coffee/guides/capsule-coffee-machines/).
- **You want café drinks and you're up for a project:** [Espresso](/coffee/guides/espresso-at-home/).

Whatever you pick, a good grinder will make more difference than the brewer. My [hand grinder review](/coffee/reviews/hand-coffee-grinders/) has a few that work for everything except espresso, and some that handle that too.
